remaining to respond to the original pleading or within 14 days after service of the amended pleading, whichever is later.

* * *

(d) Supplemental Pleadings. On motion and reasonable notice, the court may, on just terms, permit a party to serve a supplemental pleading setting out any transaction, occurrence, or event that happened after the date of the pleading to be supplemented. The court may permit supplementation even though the original pleading is defective in stating a claim or defense. The court may order that the opposing party plead to the supplemental pleading within a specified time.



•One amended pleading may be filed within 21 days of service of original pleading or responsive pleading or pre-answer motion under Rule 12

•Otherwise, amendment requires opposing party’s written consent or leave of court

•The court should freely give leave to amend when justice so requires

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON RULE 15
1. When can a party amend its pleading without asking anyone for permission? When a party needs permission to amend a pleading, whose permission does it need?

2. Consider the scenario of a plaintiff who, after a case has gone on for some time, wants to change or add allegations to her complaint. Can she do so? How might a late amendment to the complaint to add new causes of action, or even new parties, help the court reach a just result in the action? How might it impair the fairness and accuracy of the court in deciding the case?
